{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B000068NZC', 'answerTime': 'Jul 3, 2013', 'unixTime': 1372834800, 'question': 'Would a sennheiser HMD280-13 and this adapter work in a regular computer 3.5mm mic port for recording?', 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': "I'm not familiar with that headset but if it terminates to an XLR then yes, this should take that and convert it to a plug that would fit in a 3.5mm mic port. I'm not sure if the cable would let you HEAR anything, but I've been using it to come out of a shotgun mic and into my camera and its worked great."}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B000068NZG', 'answerTime': 'May 6, 2014', 'unixTime': 1399359600, 'question': 'Can this be used to connect a Shure SM48-LC Vocal Dynamic Microphone with a Yamaha Receiver RX v575?', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': "If the receiver has a 1/8 mic jack, then theoretically it will work. I do not recommend it because you will experience signal degradation when using this product. IE, the mic will be super quiet, as it is underpowered. You will need a pre-amp or sound board, which will convert from XLR input to 1/4 output, while also giving the mic enough power to pick up sounds properly. 1/4 cords can have 1/4 at one end and 1\\8 at the other, which will still maintain a good signal. You're best bet would be converting the XLR over a pre-amp or soundboard into a USB (or FireWire) output, which should plug into the receiver, or a computer. Bottom line though, is this converter sucks, and will not serve your needs."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000068NZG', 'answerTime': 'Feb 17, 2015', 'unixTime': 1424160000, 'question': 'so I have a mono shotgun mic.. I used this and my output is still mono only... anyway to fix this?', 'answer': 'Buy a 1/8" mono to 1/8" stereo adapter. Should be about a buck at your local Radio Shack or for a couple bucks on Amazon. Some cords that go XLR to 1/8" convert to stereo for you.'}
