{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B0002F6JH6', 'answerTime': 'Oct 8, 2013', 'unixTime': 1381215600, 'question': 'does anyone know the length of the second tier? I want to know if a Kronos X 88 will fit securely on it.', 'answer': 'It has the same width as the lower tier -- 21" to 37". So the width won\'t be an issue. However, the center of gravity will be higher so it won\'t be quite as stable as if you placed the Kronos on the lower tier, all other things being equal. I think if you have a sufficiently heavy keyboard on the bottom tier (to lower the center of gravity of the whole rig), then it\'ll be fine.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B0002F6JH6', 'answerTime': 'Oct 19, 2014', 'unixTime': 1413702000, 'question': 'Does anybody have experience with a 37 key board on the top tier? Will this work comfortably? My board measures 26 inches wide.', 'answer': 'Your best bet is to use a 1/4" piece of plywood cut to fit on the tier, then put the small keyboard on that, so that your overall stand width isn\'t at the minimum. The narrowest you can set the whole unit is about 22" (outside to outside.)'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B0002F6JH6', 'answerTime': 'Jul 10, 2014', 'unixTime': 1404975600, 'question': 'can you play from both sides (same time, two players) on the two-tier?', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': 'It might be a bit tighter than, say, sharing a piano, but I think it would work. It would be more a matter of how wide your keyboard is.'}
