{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B0002MSKWG', 'answerTime': 'Nov 5, 2014', 'unixTime': 1415174400, 'question': 'How does the strum plate sound? Like the Omnichord--a harp sound?', 'answer': 'There are many different instrument sounds for the strum plate just a few are guitar, banjo, piano, organ etc you program them in.'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B0002MSKWG', 'answerTime': 'Aug 21, 2014', 'unixTime': 1408604400, 'question': 'Can you play individual or combinations of strings, and, can you bend notes/chords?', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': "Matt, It's a combinations. As you see in the picture on the right side of the instrument, you slide your thumb or fingers on what looks like a grate. As you slide them it give the impression of a harp. If you tap your fingers in various places, it sounds like single strings but I don't what string name it is. The buttons on the left side are the chords, major, minor, 7th. It also come with a Melody keyboard overlay which I haven't learned as of yet. I don't know what bend notes/chords are but I have played to chords together if they were close enough. I still have a lot to learn. It does so much. I think you'll enjoy it, too."}
