{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B0002PBS6I', 'answerTime': 'Jun 25, 2013', 'unixTime': 1372143600, 'question': 'what is the difference between slinky and non slinky?', 'answer': 'Short answer: marketing. For the real story see the Ernie Ball website, but if you trust my memory... many, many moons ago there were few guitar string makers and they pretty much made the same thing- strings that helped a guitar be heard in a Big Band environment. The Ernie Ball company was one of the first to start marketing "boutique strings" that were easier for the player to fret and to bend. Compared to other strings at the time (anybody remember Black Diamonds?) the word "slinky" pretty much described how they felt.'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B0002PBS6I', 'answerTime': 'Feb 25, 2014', 'unixTime': 1393315200, 'question': "I'm looking for extra strings for my new Les Paul Special II. I'm buing guitar from amazon, it costs $149. Are this strings compatible?", 'answerType': '?', 'answer': "You're not going to damage it. Those are standard strings. I use them with all of my guitars. Trust me, you're good. If you want to ask me some questions about getting started, post your email in a response and I will happily guide you through it."}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B0002PBS6I', 'answerTime': 'Sep 2, 2014', 'unixTime': 1409641200, 'question': 'Do all 6 strings come in the "1 pack"?', 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': "All 6 of the strings are in the pack, they just put each string into its own paper envelope for easy identification. The ball-ends aren't color-coded like D'addario. Hope this helps. :)"}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B0002PBS6I', 'answerTime': 'Apr 15, 2014', 'unixTime': 1397545200, 'question': 'ok..will these strings work for me? I am just learning, bough Rocksmith, trouble tuning broke a string. I have NO idea what kind to buy, help please', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': 'I have found that the Regular Slinky (yellow) is much easier to tune in Rocksmith vs the Pink Super Slinky strings. This is especially the case with the songs with alternate tuning like Drop D. The pink will often not register notes right. They are a bit harder to bend but I think easier to grab with your fingers.'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B0002PBS6I', 'answerTime': 'Jul 14, 2014', 'unixTime': 1405321200, 'question': 'Would the Super Slinky (9-42) be good for a 25.5 inch scale guitar?I want to put them on my Kramer Pacer Classic.', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': 'Most strings will fit most scaled guitar lengths. The only thing you need be concerned about is "Intonation" and "String Height" in the changing of string gauges and different scale lengths. When you change string gauges it will require a change in string heights to accommodate the new difference in size of the string which will be a different distance from the frets. Any string will do and the Ernie Ball Regulars are an excellent choice. But, Intonation is the issue when you are dealing with any scale length. Don\'t want the string too forward or too back. Want it in the Sweet Spot for the best tone. So any adjustments to effect Intonation is done on the Bridge Saddle screws on most guitars and on yours, which will move that Sweet Spot forward or backward. There are sites that explain how to. Or can take it to a music place that does this type thing, which is about all of them.'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B0002PBS6I', 'answerTime': 'Dec 11, 2013', 'unixTime': 1386748800, 'question': 'Is an e string part of this package', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': 'Includes all 6 strings.'}
