{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B0009GUOQA', 'answerTime': 'Oct 7, 2014', 'unixTime': 1412665200, 'question': 'Is it possible to use two sets of outputs - as in run the XLR outputs to the house PA and run the line outs to a keyboard amp so band can monitor?', 'answer': 'Yes, the input signal, regardless of connector used, is present on all outputs. http://artproaudio.com/downloads/specsheets/ss_dti.pdf'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B0009GUOQA', 'answerTime': 'Dec 16, 2013', 'unixTime': 1387180800, 'question': 'Can this work as a converter also or do you have to use the same cable types for the input and the output? ex: use TRS 1/4" input to XLR output', 'answer': 'Yes it acts like a normal DI in that way.'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B0009GUOQA', 'answerTime': 'Jun 20, 2014', 'unixTime': 1403247600, 'question': 'I have an unbalanced rca from a mixer that needs to convert a balanced XLR to active speakers, will this work?', 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': 'It certainly should. This unit is nothing more than an isolation transformer... signal goes in on one side (input) and comes out 1:1 on the output. The only thing you might notice is a little bit of low frequency loss, but you can compensate with EQ. I hope this helps.'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B0009GUOQA', 'answerTime': '11 days ago', 'question': 'Will the DTI work to get rid of a minimal motor hum from a turntable?', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': 'I would say no... As THAT is a machinical source. It is only designed for what is know as "ground loop" hum. This is when a mixing board, let\'s say, and a guitar amplifier are hook up with a resulting HUM from the differences in voltages between the two. Hope that helps.'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B0009GUOQA', 'answerTime': 'Jan 9, 2015', 'unixTime': 1420790400, 'question': 'can this be used between a turntable and a phono preamp/stage via the rca jacks?', 'answerType': 'N', 'answer': 'The correct order would be turntable--&gt;preamp---&gt;DTI. Be sure to eliminate hum from phono cartridge wires running too close to TT motor. Also be sure to plug TT and preamp into the same outlet/power strip. Use quality RCA shielded cables and not some old RCA unshielded cables used for some cheap speakers.'}
