{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B000EEEYCW', 'answerTime': 'May 9, 2014', 'unixTime': 1399618800, 'question': 'Can you check intonation.For example: High E OPEN then at the twelfth fret?', 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': "Yes you can. If the 12th fret is sharp then turn the screw to the right. If flat, turn left until it in perfect tuning or close to it. I use a Babicz's bridge and Gotoh head stock tuners. This improves the tuning and intonation quite a bit. It's very easy to check the intonation with a Babicz's bridge. It has cams instead of set screws and a string saddle. Hope this helped!"}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000EEEYCW', 'answerTime': 'Jul 10, 2014', 'unixTime': 1404975600, 'question': "What's the difference btw GA-1 and GA-40? Will be used 85% on electric with jack, 15% acoustic with mic. Is one better or are they the same?", 'answer': "Hello Bob , I own the GA-40 and it so simple to use works really great every time.Now, I have a few friends that have the GA-1 which also is an outstanding value and works flawlessly also the only difference being that the GA-40 has it's buttons positioned differently.They are on the side of the screen instead of the bottom of the screen which as far as I can tell is only for looks,no difference in performance and the only difference is that the GA-40 has a slightly bigger digital display screen it's about a 1/2 inch larger.You can't go wrong with either unit they both do same thing, tune our guitars and basses and do it very well."}
