{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000HRLWN0', 'answerTime': 'Feb 24, 2014', 'unixTime': 1393228800, 'question': 'Where is this made?', 'answer': "Mine was made in Ireland. And if you'll be playing it along with a tuned instrument, be sure you can match its key or play the whistle in modulation. I have both C and D whistles since I'm not good enough to modulate. You get the full octave (including flats) by covering whole or half holes."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000HRLWN0', 'answerTime': 'Sep 4, 2013', 'unixTime': 1378278000, 'question': 'What brand is this whistle (clarke, walton, etc...)????', 'answer': "It is a Walton Irish Penny/Tin Whistle. in the Image you gave, that particular Whistle is in the Key of 'D' (Which is the lowest note you can play on it). It is the most generic key, and quite good for anyone starting out, mainly because of it's size (So I believe). I own one, and have enjoyed it every day since."}
