{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B000P5OUCE', 'answerTime': 'Sep 3, 2013', 'unixTime': 1378191600, 'question': "I have a Takamine EF341...said to be copied from Martin...I've owned it since '95 and have no intention to ever sell it. Will this bridge work for me?", 'answerType': '?', 'answer': "The EF341 comes with an uncompensated saddle (from what I've read - I don't own one) so I would stick with that. Despite what others have written I can't hear the difference between GraphTech and bone so I would try the GraphTech uncompensated saddle."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000P5OUCE', 'answerTime': 'May 30, 2013', 'unixTime': 1369897200, 'question': 'Which strings are compensated?', 'answer': 'All of them. Low E and B are set to the back edge of the saddle, D, G and high E are at the front edge, and A is in the middle.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000P5OUCE', 'answerTime': 'Mar 12, 2013', 'unixTime': 1363071600, 'question': 'Hi, I have a Epiphone EJ160E John Lennon Acoustic Electric, witch saddle should i buy for this guitar? Thanks.', 'answer': 'Your guitar has a &#8539;" saddle measure to make sure! Then the PQ-9280 graphtech saddle will work fine. You are goin to need some fine to medium grit sandpaper,follow the directions givin with graphtech saddle. Remember dont remove to much material but also do not force you should be able put it in place using just your hand.no hammers. Calipers are usefull here but not essential. KEEP YOUR OLD SADDLE AS A TEMPLATE. if your wanting to lower or higher action this is where you really need to pay close attention...as I have installed probly 1000 of them, you are goin to have to sand approx 2mm off each end then comes thickness sand until it slips in place without force then comes height only you know if the action was good and this is a crital spot for getting action perfect. Remember to use your old saddle as a template. Graphtech saddles are Great you will hear the differnce.'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B000P5OUCE', 'answerTime': 'Jan 14, 2014', 'unixTime': 1389686400, 'question': 'will this saddle work with a Yamaha fg700s', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': "I can't answer that since I've never seen the guitar. All I can suggest is that you get out your ruler (or better yet, some calipers) and see if it matches the dimensions of your old bridge. Also, recall that saddles almost always need to be sanded to fit. Just make sure it's not too small to start with."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000P5OUCE', 'answerTime': 'Mar 16, 2015', 'unixTime': 1426489200, 'question': 'I have Seagull S6 original, what saddle is the most fit? thank you', 'answer': "Sorry Richard... I bought my bridge after calling the company to verify that it was the right one but it didn't fit. Since it was a minor cash outlay I never went further. I would call them with you guitar including serial number and see what they have to say. Regards and good luck BGW"}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B000P5OUCE', 'answerTime': 'Feb 26, 2015', 'unixTime': 1424937600, 'question': 'Will this saddle work on the Epiphone Hummingbird acoustic?', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': "Don't go by brand or model. Use the dimensions on your old bridge, compare them to the new one, and count on sanding it to fit."}
