{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000SM96GE', 'answerTime': 'Mar 21, 2014', 'unixTime': 1395385200, 'question': 'Has anyone ACTUALLY hooked up mor than 10 pads to this and it worked? Is it possible to hook up 15-20 DIFFERENT pads/triggers using TRS cables?', 'answer': "Yes, but with one catch - If you have the snare and bass drum routed to the same input, you wont be able to hit them at the same time and have them both register. Perhaps if you paired up pads that would be less likely to be hit at the same time... but in my experience, playing drums really doesn't work like that..."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000SM96GE', 'answerTime': 'May 29, 2013', 'unixTime': 1369810800, 'question': 'Is every trigger stereo? i.e. are there 20 total trigger input channels, or are they mono, i.e. there are 10 total input channels?', 'answer': 'every input is TRS it works fine with two zones triggers, in all the only imput mono is the hi hat pedal input, ofcourse you need TRS cables to use this options, if dont you can use both zones. i hope this comment helps you'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B000SM96GE', 'answerTime': 'Feb 12, 2015', 'unixTime': 1423728000, 'question': "I'm trying to trigger my acoustic bass drum. have the trigger and ever thing plugged in to my pa. how do I set it up. it has midi output but my pa doe", 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': "This Trigger IO can't be used as a standalone. It needs either a computer with a trigger/sampling software or plugin, or a drum module of some sort. This device only takes the signal from the drum triggers and turns them into MIDI signal. Then this MIDI signal is sent either through a MIDI cable or USB cable to a receiving device (computer, drum module etc.) If you are trying to go straight from the bass drum to a PA (I'm guessing for a live performance) you have a couple options. One is to use a laptop or something similar to host your trigger software, then from there send out the live audio into your PA. I have done this with this box before and works great. Another is to get a different trigger box that also has drum samples built in. These, for the most part, are called drum modules. Same thing as the Trigger IO, but with drum sounds built in. I hope I clarified some things for you! Good luck!"}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000SM96GE', 'answerTime': 'Feb 12, 2015', 'unixTime': 1423728000, 'question': "how do I hook this up to an acoustic bass drum to trigger it. I have the trigger. and I can't figure out how to run this into my pa to get sound.", 'answer': 'Henry - The Trigger IO is just that - a trigger - you need a sound program - like EZ drummer loaded into a computer. You use the Alesis to trigger the sounds in the computer - you use the head phone jack from the computer as the input to your PA.. That\'s how it works - We are using that set up at our church and it works great - You have to remember - this is not like a Rolland drum module - there are no "on board" sounds..'}
{'answer': "As far as I know this is only a midi trigger unit. I have this between my drum triggers and Logic Pro. So it triggers whatever sample kit I have set up there. It's a great unit tho - really tough :)", 'asin': 'B000SM96GE', 'answerTime': '3 days ago', 'question': 'I am new to the drumming world.i have a pylepro2 now.would this allow me to have more kits? is bfd software any good?', 'questionType': 'open-ended'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000SM96GE', 'answerTime': 'May 29, 2013', 'unixTime': 1369810800, 'question': 'Do you need a special sound card? Would an onboard sound card work for this?', 'answer': "Any soundcard with midi input (about of 99.9% of the soundcards in the market)... Moreover, you don't even need a soundcard with MIDI input, since you can purchase a MIDI to USB converter (extremely cheap piece of gear), and plug it directly to the USB-IN of your PC/notebook. If your soundcard doesn't have ASIO drivers, which is common (in mac the drivers are different), you download the ASIO4ALL drivers, which will work teaming with any soundcard at all, and are free, BTW."}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B000SSMJG2', 'answerTime': 'Feb 23, 2015', 'unixTime': 1424678400, 'question': 'Do these effect the quality of the sound in any way?', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': 'It does affect the snare and symbols most. I bought them to be able to practice without disturbing neighbors. You can strike as hard as you normally play and experience an 80% reduction in volume. That said, you would never perform using the pads due to sound quality.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000SSMJG2', 'answerTime': 'Jan 10, 2014', 'unixTime': 1389340800, 'question': 'How do you attach the 22" bass drum pad? Velcro, tape or some other mechanism? Can you estimate how much these pads dampen the sound? Thanks.', 'answer': "There's a single velcro disc about the size of a quarter. I left my pad in place continuously for six months and the adhesive on the drum head gave out. Now I use three office binder clips on the rim -- works fine. I'd say they cut the volume by half, maybe 2/3. It's just a dull thud that doesn't carry like resonating tom would. Everyone in the house will know when you play, but a couple rooms away with doors closed they'll be able to watch TV, etc."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000SSMJG2', 'answerTime': 'Nov 4, 2014', 'unixTime': 1415088000, 'question': 'Does it come with 2 for the hi-hats (1 for top and bottom)? Or do the hats have to stay closed the whole time for it to work?', 'answer': "No. I used it for another cymbal. Then used an extra circular pad by cutting a hole in the center and pushing it down over the top hi-hat. Also folded another extra pad in half and wedged it in between top and bottom hi-hats. I'm able to use the hi-hat pedal like this as well."}
