{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000SZVZ74', 'answerTime': 'Dec 9, 2014', 'unixTime': 1418112000, 'question': 'Is your Boom wobbly when you put it in the desk clamp? It has too much room in the mounting hole, it wobbles from side to side, is that normal?', 'answer': 'Actually, there must be something extraordinary taking place. I am using the clamp as you mentioned and it is a relatively tight fit without a lot of wobble. There is supposed to be a brass/bronze bushing in the hole in the clamp, if it is no there then it would be very wobbly. Hope this helps.'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B000SZVZ74', 'answerTime': 'Sep 30, 2014', 'unixTime': 1412060400, 'question': 'I cannot clamp it to the type of desk I use. I have 3/8" &amp; 5/8" hole in my desk, can I mount it in one of these holes without using the clamp?', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': 'Buy the FL-2 mount from Heli... $28 on their site. I have used one for years. As with their mics it is well constructed. They have other solutions as well...http://www.heilsound.com/amateur/products/booms-and-hardwars/hardware'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000SZVZ74', 'answerTime': 'Feb 17, 2015', 'unixTime': 1424160000, 'question': 'I received my Heil Sound PL-2T as new, and it seemed to have been used/worn and it did not come with a clamp? Its suppose to come with clamp right?', 'answer': 'Yes'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000SZVZ74', 'answerTime': 'Feb 23, 2014', 'unixTime': 1393142400, 'question': 'How long is the distance from the bottom of the mount to the mic mount? I would like an exact measurement...', 'answer': "I don't think I quite understand your question but I will answer as best I can. From the mount to the elbow is 21.75 inches. (Outer dimensions) From the elbow to the mic mount is 20.0 inches Folded and fully closed as in the pic from the mount clamp horizontally out to the mic is 6.0 inches From the horizontal plane at the mount top up to the mic mount in the closed position is about 4.0 inches fully extended out is 38.5 but pointing to the sky. The problem with this mount for me is the hinge point at the mount is limited to maybe 80 degrees. The angle you see in the pic is the extent of its travel in the other direction. The center hinge to the mic part has more travel maybe 140 degrees. This was disappointing to me, as I wanted to mount the boom on a wall and pull it out and down into place when needed. I also wanted the boom and mic to go straight back and flat against the wall. So if you were to draw a vertical line straight up from the mount to the center hinge the mic can only get to about 18 inches from the wall. So though they call this an overhead boom it did not work for me, but would make a good desktop mounted boom."}
