{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B00126HUGS', 'answerTime': 'Jan 28, 2015', 'unixTime': 1422432000, 'question': 'Can another clarinet peg screw in to the piccolo threads? I already have the single stand for my Bb, but will be playing a C and A in a concert.', 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': 'Dear Kathryn: I have returned the stand to Amazon. If memory serves, my stand had 3 holes with the same size threads in all of them, but since I cannot be sure I do not want to mislead you. My stand had two flute pegs, which would serve for two clarinets, and then presumably you could bring your other clarinet peg, and put it in the piccolo spot. A clarinet player friend of my tried the stand and liked it. I would say the larger pegs are more compatible with the clarinet than with the flute, which as you can see, i highly recommend against using with the flute.'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B00126HUGS', 'answerTime': 'Dec 4, 2014', 'unixTime': 1417680000, 'question': 'Will this stand hold an Alto Flute?', 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': "It *can*, but I wouldnt. The peg is too narrow, and the flute wobbles. Having said that, the base is heavy enough to keep it upright, but the wobbling makes me uncomfortable. It'd be too distracting to have on stage or in a pit with me. Someone else might be able to suggest a different peg; most (all?) Hercules pegs are interchangeable."}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B00126HUGS', 'answerTime': 'Nov 3, 2014', 'unixTime': 1415001600, 'question': 'can this hold a flute with a b foot?', 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': "Yes. The diameter of the larger pegs is sufficiently thick to keep a flute stable, with or without a b foot's added length. The smaller peg is really only useful for a piccolo."}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B00126HUGS', 'answerTime': 'Jun 30, 2014', 'unixTime': 1404111600, 'question': 'Could one of them hold an alto flute?', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': 'The flute and the alto flute seem to have the same foot joint, however the length and shape of the alto flute might necessitate a longer neck on the stand in order to accommodate balance problems. Hope this helps.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B00126HUGS', 'answerTime': 'Jan 23, 2014', 'unixTime': 1390464000, 'question': 'I would like to use one of the pegs for my alto flute. Is that feasible or will it fall slightly to one side like you described for the oboe? Thanks', 'answer': "My stand came with a piccolo peg, a flute peg and a clarinet peg, none of which will exactly fit an alto flute. Hercules makes a variety of pegs and they can be mixed and matched as desired; however, I don't know if an alto flute peg is available."}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B00126HUGS', 'answerTime': 'Nov 24, 2013', 'unixTime': 1385280000, 'question': "Can the extra flute/clarinet peg be detached? I only need the flute/piccolo stand part so it isn't needed but I don't want to unbalance the stand.", 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': 'Yes, all 3 pegs can be attached or detached...they\'re separate. The stand is VERY sturdy...you won\'t need to worry about the stand being unbalanced. The "clarinet" peg is actually the same as the flute peg (there are 2 flute/clarinet pegs and a piccolo peg).'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B00126HUGS', 'answerTime': 'Oct 3, 2014', 'unixTime': 1412319600, 'question': "What are the diameters of the pegs, both at the part that gets inserted into the instrument's bore and at the base?", 'answer': 'One peg is very thin (flute, piccolo, Eb clarinet). The other 2 are standard size (Bb, A Clarinet). I am guessing a couple of trumpets could go on the larger pegs.'}
