{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B0018Z7EIM', 'answerTime': 'Dec 10, 2013', 'unixTime': 1386662400, 'question': 'I\'ve been noticing reviewers speaking about "burn in". What is required with these buds?', 'answer': "I've happily owned a pair of these phones for a while, and use them daily. I'm aware of burn-in considerations, especially as I used to mess around with high end speakers and drivers years ago. My experience with these exact phones is that the bass notes did show some increased quickness within a couple of weeks. It was nothing earth shattering, but I remember noting that the bass response got a little more responsive as the phones broke-in. I do not believe that the Sennheisers need a formal break in with white noise generators or any such tools. It's just that you'll notice that you like them even a little more after the initial listening period. What I've noticed also is that I'm accustomed to the sound of these phones and when I use a different set, I'm not at all happy with the strange phones. The Sennheisers deliver a sound quality that is very attractive to me. They were a worthwhile investment in my way of looking at it. Good Luck with your decision."}
{'answer': 'Yes, you can but they don\'t have a remote volume or play/pause contrrol. In addition an "iPod" headphone has a contoured Eq. meaning that music will be more pleasant opposite to In ear monitors headphones that bring "a flat frequency response " as they are disigned to translate music or speech exactly as it is without coloring.', 'asin': 'B0018Z7EIM', 'answerTime': '18 days ago', 'question': 'Can I plug these into my ipod? What does it mean that they are for "wireless monitor applications"?', 'questionType': 'open-ended'}
