{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B002Q0WT6U', 'answerTime': 'Jun 27, 2014', 'unixTime': 1403852400, 'question': 'how does this rosin compare to Andrea? Dust wise and sound quality?', 'answer': "Not having used Andrea, I couldn't say about the comparison. Jade, made in England, is one of the two rosins I have used on violin bows (both baroque and modern) for years. It is superior as fas as dust is concerned and only occaisionally do I have to wipe down the strings. It is worth the $12 or so to try it and see how you like it."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B002Q0WT6U', 'answerTime': 'May 19, 2014', 'unixTime': 1400482800, 'question': 'How is this rosin for bluegrass and old time?', 'answer': 'It is okay for most applications, however, I found it did not last long enough to maintain the "bite" needed for fiddling. I suggest you try something "softer", but watch the residue build up. You could try a cello rosin. I haven\'t tried it yet. I use Bernadel violin rosin, and like it very much.'}
