{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B002TP9154', 'answerTime': 'Jan 16, 2015', 'unixTime': 1421395200, 'question': 'Does this work on a Stanton T.92 turntable? With which cartridge?', 'answer': 'Yes it can be used with the T.92, and with any "headshell-mountable" cartridge (not the "plug-in" type.)'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B002TP9154', 'answerTime': 'May 3, 2013', 'unixTime': 1367564400, 'question': 'Does is come with plastic clips to insulate the cartridge from the headshell?', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': 'Depends on what you mean by "insulate". The headshell is conductive, but is coated with non-conductive paint. The only way you can tell it\'s conductive is that there is continuity between the cartridge mount screws, the extra weight screw, and the shield ring where the headshell mounts to the tone arm. There is no continuity between the shell and any of the four signal wires. HTH'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B002TP9154', 'answerTime': 'Jan 8, 2013', 'unixTime': 1357632000, 'question': 'material - metal/plastic', 'answer': 'I cannot tell from the text whether you are asking "which is it, metal or plastic?" or "which metal and/or plastic is it?" I cannot answer the second, so will do my best with the first. It appears to be non-conductive paint or plastic over a metal shell. I measure electrical continuity between the added weight screw thread (between the elongated cartridge mount holes) and the rear connector mounting. The metal is not iron; it does not attract a magnet. I hope this helps.'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B002TP500E', 'answerTime': 'Dec 13, 2013', 'unixTime': 1386921600, 'question': 'Will this work with Traktor pro and mac?', 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': 'Yes as long as you install software that comes with and configure Traktor Pro.'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B002TP500E', 'answerTime': 'Dec 11, 2013', 'unixTime': 1386748800, 'question': 'will it work with mac?', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': "It works with my Mac, but to be completely honest it's a little temperamental. I suggest downloading Virtual DJ LE, there's a version that is specifically built for the Stanton SC 3 System. It's hard getting used to, and you spend your time looking down at the console making sure you're in the right place to do the things you need to do. There are hardly any physical cues to tell you where you are, one surface feeling exactly like the next. That having been said, it's like learning how to type. You can get along without knowing, just hunting and pecking as you please. But once you learn how, you'll never go back. The same goes for this system. It's still a conversation starter, and you can do amazing things with it. Hope I helped more than I confused. :-/"}
