{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B002TREG34', 'answerTime': 'Jun 17, 2014', 'unixTime': 1402988400, 'question': 'my husband bought me a signed Fender, Squier, bullet start. which model should I buy', 'answer': "I hung up my Strat with Woodies Hanger: The Ultimate Guitar Hanger - SM-01 Standard Model. Should work great. Had mine up for 2 years now and it's still up there solid and easy to take on and off."}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B002TREG34', 'answerTime': 'Dec 15, 2013', 'unixTime': 1387094400, 'question': 'Does this work with Acoustic Guitars?', 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': 'Hi Tim, I have never hung an acoustic guitar with the"Woodies" hangers. But I have hung Fenders, Gibson LP and 335, Rickenbacker and a Fender Bass. These all worked out great. I would thing if you can get enough spacing froim the wall it will work. Bill'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B002TREG34', 'answerTime': 'Nov 25, 2013', 'unixTime': 1385366400, 'question': 'Would this work with a guitar with Straplocks installed? What type might work best? Schaller? Dunlop? Etc...', 'answer': 'Basically, the answer is yes! Dunlop Strap locks snap into the outside of the strap button, so that leaves the groove free to rest in the hanger, but you would still have pop the straplocks out, then hang the guitar and then reinsert the strap locks, if you wanted the strap attached while it is hanging. Otherwise, the simple answer, since both Schaller and Dunlop strap locks make it easy to take the strap off, just detach the strap before you hang it, then either will work on the Woodies Hanger.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B002TUTUIM', 'answerTime': 'Oct 23, 2014', 'unixTime': 1414047600, 'question': 'will the harmonizer work without playing a guitar at the same time? will it work simply by singing through it?', 'answer': 'Just set it to Manual\' and choose a key. Everything you sing will be harmony parts in that key ONLY. If you understand harmony (a little theory here...), then you will realize that certain notes in the scale will produce a \'fixed\' harmony that might not be appropriate for the chord that you are singing over. For example, if you choose Manual mode in the key of \'C\' and an \'F Major\' chord is sounding, and you sing a \'C\' note, the harmony above ("High" setting) will sound an E, making the resulting vocal \'chord\' an F-Major-7th. Playing guitar with the same situation NOT in manual mode will result in the harmony part above sounding the \'F\' note, the correct harmony in context of the F chord.'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B002TUTUIM', 'answerTime': 'Sep 22, 2014', 'unixTime': 1411369200, 'question': 'Can you turn the guitar off on the out put for recording vocal harmony? And can you record just the harmony vocals with out the main vocals coming thr', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': "Yes, of course... Simply turn the 'Guitar' volume knob all the way down. It doesn't change the 'input' volume or presence of the guitar. I do this all the time and it works great! Also, for 'just harmony', rotate the 'Harmony' knob all the way to the right, and only harmonies will come out. However, why not use your 'main vocal' as one of the harmony parts when over-dubbing? This can 'thicken' the harmonies... Also, to give the harmonies presence, perform them twice into two separate tracks and pan each hard-left/right, respectively and delay one side about 6-to-8 ms and listen to the magic!"}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B002TUTUIM', 'answerTime': 'Mar 28, 2014', 'unixTime': 1395990000, 'question': 'so it works with keyboards? (1/4 line in)', 'answer': 'Yes it should work with your keyboard but if that is your primary use I would recommend getting the midi version of it. That will give you a higher accuracy level on the harmonys'}
