{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B002TUTUIM', 'answerTime': 'Sep 30, 2012', 'unixTime': 1348988400, 'question': 'Questions from a novice at this: Is there any way to output directly into my computer or must I have a separate microphone plugged into my USB port, and this plugged into speakers?', 'answer': "Bernadette, I don't have this particular item, but own TC Electronics VoiceLive Touch Vocal Effects Processor and plug my iphone/ipod into it, connect to an amp, connect the TC Electronics Vocal Microphone MP-75 Dynamic Microphone, Super-Cardiod to it and sing along with my own tunes. My friends and I have a blast with the different voice settings. I am no pro by any means, this was all in the name of having fun. Have not tried connecting my computer yet, to try and access my itunes account. Initially, I was looking for something for my computer, but stumbled onto this and have no regrets."}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B002TUTUIM', 'answerTime': 'Oct 23, 2014', 'unixTime': 1414047600, 'question': 'Can I plug a keyboard?', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': "It won't hurt it... Try it. I have plugged in my Yamaha PSR into it with piano, guitar, and other, natural multi-timbrel sounds, and it works. Just make sure the input levels are correct for both instrument and voice. (Actually, in this case, MIDI does not relate since all of the inputs on the TC-Helicon Voicetone-G XT harmonizer has only analog I/O (except for the USB connector for preset control using TC-Helicon's application.)"}
