{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B0036R4SV6', 'answerTime': 'Oct 7, 2013', 'unixTime': 1381129200, 'question': 'Is this wire the same as used for forming fish hooks?', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': 'I am guessing that it is a trade secret. However, it is high carbon steel and can be tempered, like a fish hook. We use it in picture framing for holding 3-d objects in place in shadowbox framing. We use polyolefin heat shrink tubing over the exposed ends so as to not damage the object being framed.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B0036R4SV6', 'answerTime': 'Apr 22, 2014', 'unixTime': 1398150000, 'question': 'How bendable is this wire when comparing it to, lets say, a clothes hanger?', 'answer': 'This is a very bendable wire, but strong.'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B0036R4SSO', 'answerTime': 'Apr 7, 2014', 'unixTime': 1396854000, 'question': 'Does the wire have memory, ie, is it hard to straighten?', 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': 'It is easily straightened by clamping one end in a vise and twisting 3-4 twists per inch with pliers or vise-grips while pulling it straight.'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B0036R4SSO', 'answerTime': 'Apr 4, 2014', 'unixTime': 1396594800, 'question': 'Does this wire holds its shape? I need 1mm wire to make pushrods for my rc plane.', 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': 'Yup, its a little bit on the heavy side but I use it for all my planes that only need short pushrods. FT Bloody Wonder, FT 3D etc.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B0036R4SSO', 'answerTime': 'Feb 1, 2014', 'unixTime': 1391241600, 'question': 'What is the product weight? Amazon lists 1.6oz, but I compute 4oz by density.', 'answer': "Mines weighing 3.8oz and ive used a few feet, so I'd imagine you'd be correct with your calculation of 4oz. Hope this helps."}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B0036R4SSO', 'answerTime': 'Apr 4, 2014', 'unixTime': 1396594800, 'question': 'Does this wire holds its shape? I need 1mm or .037in wire to make pushrods for my rc plane', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': "Its rigid wire. I don't know if it would hold up on a rc plane though."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B0036R4SSO', 'answerTime': 'Mar 2, 2015', 'unixTime': 1425283200, 'question': 'Lets say i got this wire and hung 500 pounds off of it would it hold?', 'answer': 'I\'m not a mechanical engineer, but based on the specifications given in the description, I would say it depends. According to the description: Tensile strength - 303-333 ksi (min. - max.) Tensile Strength Max 335000 PSI Outside Diameter 0.051 inches So, the two tensile strengths are in slight disagreement. If you go with the lower value for safety, then it supports 303ksi (which I\'m assuming means kilopounds/square inch). With a diameter of .051 inches, that works out to a cross section of: pi * diameter = 3.14 * .051 = .16 square inches Assuming tensile strength scales linearly with the cross section (it probably doesn\'t), then the total amount the wire should be able to support before failing is roughly: .016 square inches * 303 kilopounds/square inch = 48 kilopounds Which is far more than necessary to support 500lbs. The "depends" part comes from the fact that I\'m not an engineer, and don\'t fully understand the forces at work. Be careful, and don\'t use it for anything safety-critical unless you get a real engineer to evaluate it!'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B0036R4SSO', 'answerTime': 'Feb 27, 2015', 'unixTime': 1425024000, 'question': 'how thick is it? Is it as thick as a Quarter or thicker?', 'answer': 'No, it is about as big around as the lead in a number two pencil'}
