{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B0038MTH8S', 'answerTime': 'Dec 6, 2012', 'unixTime': 1354780800, 'question': 'Wattage: If I have a Vox AC15C1 Custom Series Tube Amp, how many people could I play with my band?', 'answer': 'You could def blow the noise off the roof of a small coffee shop. And worst case scenario you can Microphone the amp into a PA system and get as loud as you want. Or use the output line to connect an even bigger Amp Cab.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B0038MTH8S', 'answerTime': 'Feb 1, 2015', 'unixTime': 1422777600, 'question': "It's loud enough for a live concert o gig in a bar?", 'answer': "Unless you are playing in a touring band that does shows to audiences 500 plus or desire to lose your hearing at an early age, the AC15 is all you need. Sure AC30's are legendary, but this thing breaks up at a lower volume and delivers a warm tone without deafening your audience at smaller venues. It's easier to carry around as well. Any decent venue mics your amp anyway. This is an excellent little workhorse amp for the price. Be forewarned the reverb is not anything special."}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B0038MTH8S', 'answerTime': 'Jan 26, 2015', 'unixTime': 1422259200, 'question': 'Can I use the "normal channel" as an auxiliary input? I woul like to be able to play a track on my phone through the amp while also playing the guitar', 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': "I never tried that, probably because when I was in my first band (late '60's) we started off broke and actually had to play two guitars through one amp at one point. It sounded like crap! That said, you can use both inputs simultaneously, so what you want to do certainly should work."}
