{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B0039PPW60', 'answerTime': 'Dec 6, 2013', 'unixTime': 1386316800, 'question': 'Can I use it with Logic Pro X?', 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': "You can use it as an audio interface, but you can't use it as a controller. It'll mix everything coming into the mixer down to a stereo mix and send that through the usb interface."}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B0039PPW60', 'answerTime': 'Oct 17, 2014', 'unixTime': 1413529200, 'question': 'Im interested in buying this. We have 6 mics, and need to record the audio from all of them to a laptop during meetings. Will this do what I need?', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': 'It depends on two things - 1: what kind of mics you have and 2: if you need all of the audio recorded into separate channels on the computer. If you have 6 mics that are XLR or need phantom power, then this will not suit your needs - this can only power 4 XLR mics. (If you have what are called dynamic mics with 1/4" plugs - or self powered condensor mics that can use 1/4" jacks - then you are fine). For the second part (about separate channels) it really depends what you want to be able to do. The USB part of this mixer records just two separate channels into the computer - the same as the left and right main output of the mixer. So if you were to use 6 mics you would have them all recorded, but all together. (Thus, if someone was too quiet, you could not just turn them up after the fact.) You can use the panning to get two completely separate tracks, but that\'s clearly not enough for what you\'re trying to do. If you want to record all 6 tracks independently you will need something more expensive. I hope this helps!'}
