{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B003FRMRC4', 'answerTime': 'Oct 11, 2013', 'unixTime': 1381474800, 'question': 'Does the volume change with how hard or soft you hit the keys?', 'answer': 'Yes, somewhat you can tell when playing piano and forte. However, this models volume at its loudest is okay for practice play but, if used in an actual venue external speakers will be necessary. On a scale of 1-10 the volume is probably about a 6.5'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B003FRMRC4', 'answerTime': 'Dec 27, 2013', 'unixTime': 1388131200, 'question': 'Is it worth the money ?', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': "This is a very good beginner's keyboard. I'm a beginner and am quite satisfied with its features and performance. It has a large library of synthesized effects that are easy to access, as well as a tone-bending lever. It does not have a sustain pedal, which is probably a necessity and I purchased separately. The instruction manual is comprehensive, but covers the instrument's features only and is not particularly useful as a guide to learning to play. I purchased Suzuki keyboard books, and am currently working on organizing my learning using these and some downloaded instruction materials. Two final points only: (1) the kit includes a connecting cable and software for a computer interface; this will probably be quite useful but is way beyond what I can use at this time; (2) the kit also includes headphones, which are also quite useful but are almost always going to be wasted value, since it's inconceivable that someone purchasing this instrument won't already have their own headphones."}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B003FRMRC4', 'answerTime': 'Dec 26, 2013', 'unixTime': 1388044800, 'question': 'can you use a pedal?', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': 'You can buy a pedal separately and it connects to the keyboard via the "sustain" receptacle on the back of the keyboard. It works well'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B003FRMRC4', 'answerTime': 'Nov 25, 2013', 'unixTime': 1385366400, 'question': 'Can I plug a microphone directly into the keyboard?', 'answerType': 'N', 'answer': 'No you cannot. The only ports in the back of the keyboard are a sustain pedal, a MIDI USB cable, and headphones.'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B003FRMRC4', 'answerTime': 'Dec 24, 2013', 'unixTime': 1387872000, 'question': 'Is it good for beginners?', 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': 'The 76-key piano is perfect for beginners. This model comes highly recommended by music teachers and music stores alike. My son has had this for about 3 months now and is doing great learning how to master this keyboard.'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B003FRMRC4', 'answerTime': 'Dec 18, 2013', 'unixTime': 1387353600, 'question': 'Are the keys weighted?', 'answerType': 'N', 'answer': 'The keys are not weighted, but they give some resistance to replicate that feel.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B003FVB7XA', 'answerTime': 'Mar 31, 2014', 'unixTime': 1396249200, 'question': "How do you add external speakers? I haven't found any pic of the back side of keyboard. I'm not buying until I know...", 'answer': 'It\'s easy. On the back there is a 1/4" jack for outside audio. You can plug in earphones or send the signal to an external audio amp. I totally love my YPG-235. I am handicapped by stroke -- my left side is non-functioning, so I play the left hand chords with my right hand and save that in the memory. I playback the memory and add the right hand to it -- works great. It\'s a marvelous piece of good engineering. Dave George'}
