{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B003H91JDS', 'answerTime': 'Apr 3, 2015', 'unixTime': 1428044400, 'question': 'can this power a 700mah boomerang looper pedal?', 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': 'Alaska Guy, Your Boomerang will only draw about 400ma, you might want to consider using the Pedal Power AC. Alternatively you can power it with a Pedal Power 2+ from outputs 5 and 6 with the Dip Switches set to Normal using a Current Doubler Adapter and a Reverse Polarity Barrel Cable. Best wishes! -Pro Audio Land'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B003H91JDS', 'answerTime': 'Mar 17, 2015', 'unixTime': 1426575600, 'question': 'could you power an amp with the 3 prong outlet in the back of the unit?', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': "An amplifier has a high current draw. I wouldn't plug an amp in there. The connector on the power supply is referred to as the 'courtesy' and is meant to connect smaller things like another voodoo labs power supply or perhaps a power strip (to connect 9-12v adapters to power pedals)"}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B003H91JDS', 'answerTime': 'Mar 10, 2015', 'unixTime': 1425970800, 'question': 'Is the 9v power supply limited to only 4 slots? Or can u plug in pedals requiring 9v power to higher voltage slots?', 'answer': "There are four outputs at 9v, one of which is a higher current supply shared with a 12v output (the total current available is shared between them). The last output is 18v. As to whether your pedals will work in the higher voltage outputs: That depends on the pedal. Analog signal pedals (like most overdrives and boosts and the like) should be fine. They may even sound better. However, if you've got a digital pedal our am analog synth module, you should stick to the 9v outputs."}
