{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B0045I3JLK', 'answerTime': 'Dec 10, 2013', 'unixTime': 1386662400, 'question': 'Can I use this with a bass or is there a different pedal for the bass?', 'answer': 'Hello. You can use the pedal with guitar or bass. Just make sure that the ps-6 is first in the chain so it picks up the signal well.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B0045I3JLK', 'answerTime': 'Jan 11, 2013', 'unixTime': 1357891200, 'question': 'Does it work only with single notes? Or does it also work with chords?', 'answer': "I'm not saying it won't work for chords but I don't think it does it very well, I think it's main priority is to create the illusion of a second guitarist for dual octave lead play. For about $50 more you can get a lexicon MX 200 that in my opinion does everything way better and it's not just a harmony pedal,it's a multi FX rackmount unit but it's lightweight and very simple to use. The harmony FX on it to me are much easier to use and sound a lot more correctly blended than the Boss Harmonist. You definitely get way way more for the money with the lexicon versus the boss,that is a promise."}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B0045I3JLK', 'answerTime': 'Mar 28, 2015', 'unixTime': 1427526000, 'question': 'Can this be used with mic? Need Darth Vader voice', 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': "I kinda doubt it because you have to select the key that a song is in for this pedal to harmonize. Saying that, you don't really talk in a key. Watch demo video on YouTube to see how this pedal works. I would buy a product the would actually alter my voice as advertised rather than taking a chance with this pedal."}
