{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B004T0BM0O', 'answerTime': 'Feb 3, 2015', 'unixTime': 1422950400, 'question': 'I see that these can be split and run off the same controller; can you split w/ a gap (connecting cable/ cerro wire) and do the same?', 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': 'Yes, the cable is very clearly marked where it can be cut. There are Y connectors and special cables available for this purpose. You will need to do some soldering. Shrink tubing lined with adhesive is a good thing to have as well to protect the solder joints. It is usually called waterproof or marine grade shrink tubing.'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B004T0BM0O', 'answerTime': 'Jan 11, 2013', 'unixTime': 1357891200, 'question': 'can one remote work with two sets?', 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': 'Yes, I bought three sets and one remote controls the two sets I installed.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B004T0NFCC', 'answerTime': 'Apr 21, 2014', 'unixTime': 1398063600, 'question': 'What size is the drum pad? The band director has requested a 10" or larger practice pad', 'answer': 'It is 8". As a percussion teacher with music ed background, I wonder why the band director has requested 10" or larger. 8" is standard and in my opinion preferable to a larger pad for a beginner because the smaller pad keeps students focused on staying in the center. A larger pad tends to promote sloppiness and "flailing" for beginners. Hope this isn\'t too late.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B004T0NFCC', 'answerTime': 'Apr 4, 2013', 'unixTime': 1365058800, 'question': 'The picture shows a kit but the parts list sounds as if the drum pad and drumsticks are not part of it. What exactly is included?', 'answer': 'Sticks, bells, drum pad, and case. I had the same question originally and after a couple of calls a better list of information was sent. VERY PLEASED with the set. One thing you should know the drum sticks were a step up from a beginners level so I did have to purchase another set from Amazon..$7.50 We saved over $250.00 purchasing it this way vs. purchasing it though the representative that came to the school. Hope that helps!'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B004T0NFCC', 'answerTime': 'Dec 13, 2013', 'unixTime': 1386921600, 'question': 'How much does this weigh? How do the bells attach to the stand? Do the bells have a case or are the bell notes exposed in the bag?', 'answer': 'Not sure of exact weight...guestimate it to be around 30lbs. It is on wheels. I bought for my 11 year old daughter who is a "bean pole" without much muscle mass but she has no problems transporting it. Bells are totally enclosed with inner flap to further protect the. Bells have a hand sized locking "nut" that secure it to the stand. This is one feature that the Pearl is much better than the Ludwig bells that some of my students have (which just "sit" on the stand). Good question. Hope this helps.'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B004T0NFCC', 'answerTime': 'Sep 3, 2013', 'unixTime': 1378191600, 'question': 'Are the bell mallet tips made out of plastic?', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': "They are wood, but need glue-- easy-- and cute set with sturdy rolling bag. You will prob end up getting new mallets eventually (year 2) but it's not a big deal."}
