{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B005PGGU9O', 'answerTime': 'Nov 15, 2013', 'unixTime': 1384502400, 'question': 'does it make your guitar sound like a banjo', 'answerType': 'N', 'answer': "No, it doesn't. A guitar capo increases the key you're playing in one half step for every fret higher you move it. E -&gt; F on the first fret or E -&gt; F# on the second fret The higher up you move the capo, the brighter and more 'tinny' the sound of your guitar becomes, and on the higher registers one might be able to compare the sound to a ukulele, however I wouldn't say that it would make your guitar sound like a banjo."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B005PGGU9O', 'answerTime': 'Dec 6, 2013', 'unixTime': 1386316800, 'question': 'dose this work on plane acoustic guitar Rogue left handed . ?', 'answer': 'It should work on any acoustic.'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B005PGGU9O', 'answerTime': 'Jan 11, 2014', 'unixTime': 1389427200, 'question': 'does this work on ukulele?', 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': "The Ukes fretboard is less wide and a hair less thick so, yes it will work, but this is not a well made capo. My Uke is a Kala KA-MT. Check out the black Keyser cap here on amazon or the monoprice capo at the website of that same name. I have all of these and just saw the Keyser in black for about ten dollars. That's the deal."}
