{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B005TPM1NK', 'answerTime': 'Nov 20, 2013', 'unixTime': 1384934400, 'question': 'How do you-all think this amp would work w/a solid-body electric (I have an Ibanez AF75)? Thx!', 'answer': "John, This is a great amp for acoustic, and it has a lot of very cool features, including the ability for two inputs. I have used it for acoustic and vocals for practice and small gigs, as well as for acoustic and electric playing with a band. The sound for acoustic (I have a Martinn D35) is great, but less so for electric solid body. It's adequate, but not great. I now use a Fender Champion 100 for my electric and the Fishman for acoustic. BTW, the Fishman beats the heck out of the Fender for acoustic. Hope that helps."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B005TPM1NK', 'answerTime': 'Feb 4, 2015', 'unixTime': 1423036800, 'question': 'The XLR Outputs on the back of this amplifier, are they male or female? In relation, what type of XLR cable should I buy (male or female)? Thanks.', 'answer': 'Hi there. All are male. Channels 1 and 2 have a D.I. pre-EQ out. , PLUS a 1/4" send and return EFX loop. There is also a general Mix D.I. out for both channels. Additionally: Aux. inputs for 1/8" and 1/4" - I haven\'t had occasion to use these, but I imagine one could plug in an iPod here, and finally, a footswitch, which I elected to not purchase. All XLR\'s are male, all others are female. Are you running the Loudbox through a larger system? If so, a direct box (D.I.) is usually used due to the separate power sources. I hope this helps.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B005TPM1NK', 'answerTime': 'Aug 22, 2014', 'unixTime': 1408690800, 'question': 'Can two guitar players plug into this amp and have two microphones plugged in as well so they can both play and sing simultaneously.', 'answer': "You can't plug more than two things into the front (guitars or microphones or one of each). However, you could plug two guitars into the front and plug your microphones into the auxiliary in the back through a separate pre-amp or similar device."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B005TPM1NK', 'answerTime': 'Feb 1, 2014', 'unixTime': 1391241600, 'question': 'I am getting very loud feedback. Volume is at 1/4. Feedback control helps a bit at lower volume. I am using an LR Baggs M80 soundhole pickup.', 'answer': 'you are sending too much energy to the pickup from the amp, so cut back on the pickup input and increase the main to get your volume, or you are too close or in front of the speaker, move things around a bit. if it is a high frequency feedback lower the treble knob to eliminate feed back. if none of these work you need an eq between the pickup and the amp. good luck'}
