{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B006Z89FUM', 'answerTime': 'Feb 18, 2014', 'unixTime': 1392710400, 'question': 'I have never gotten to play a keytar so I have a few questions. 1. Does this product have different sounds on it? Like different instruments?', 'answer': "This particular one has no sounds, it's just a MIDI controller, set up to control another keyboard that you would already have."}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B006Z89FUM', 'answerTime': 'Aug 20, 2014', 'unixTime': 1408518000, 'question': 'Can the touch sensitivity be removed', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': 'The velocity, or touch sensitivity on the pads can be adjusted from inside the vyzex software editor that comes with it. As for the keys, most DAW software should have a way to control the input or output velocity. The unit itself does not appear to have this ability. Hope this helps!'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B006Z89FUM', 'answerTime': 'Nov 5, 2014', 'unixTime': 1415174400, 'question': 'can i hook it up to an amp', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': "Not really. It doesn't have any sounds on its own. You can use MIDI to hook up the Vortex to another keyboard, and hook that keyboard up to an amp."}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B006Z89FUM', 'answerTime': 'Feb 5, 2014', 'unixTime': 1391587200, 'question': 'Does it come with an AC adapter?', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': 'No. It only comes with a USB adapter, but if you plug that into a computer and set the power switch to "USB," the keytar will receive power from the computer and you won\'t need batteries for it.'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B006Z89FUM', 'answerTime': 'Jun 11, 2014', 'unixTime': 1402470000, 'question': 'Does this keytar have a output for sound system? To play live at venue', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': "The Keytar is just a midi-controller, so you'll still need a computer to actually produce sounds. (So, the music would come out of the computer; the keytar doesn't have sound outputs.)"}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B006Z89FUM', 'answerTime': 'Nov 5, 2014', 'unixTime': 1415174400, 'question': 'can i hook it up to an amp', 'answerType': 'N', 'answer': 'The vortex does not produce any sound on its own. The Vortex sends messages to another device (another keyboard, rack mount device) that produces sound. The messages are sent via the MIDI (Musical Instrument Digital Interface) standard. So you do not hook up the Vortex to an amp. You hook up the Vortex to a devices that produces sound (digital piano for example) and THAT device is hookup up to an amp. think of the Vortex as a remote keyboard for another device, not as a sound producing device itself. Hope this helps.'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B006Z89FUM', 'answerTime': 'Oct 7, 2014', 'unixTime': 1412665200, 'question': 'Thinking about purchasing the Alisis Vortex keytar but have no experience with a midi connection to my Yamaha MM8. Is this a difficult proceedure?', 'answerType': 'N', 'answer': 'Should not be difficult. I would recommend that you read the MM8 manual, and look for how to send program change , most significant bit (MSB) and least significant bit (LSB). I own a PX-5S, and to control stage settings I send MSB 112, no LSB, and Program Set = the stage setting I want.'}
