{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B007R9B0UQ', 'answerTime': 'Aug 5, 2014', 'unixTime': 1407222000, 'question': "Does this have ground wire the fact the 120's didn't was a noise issue", 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': 'Yes, it has a ground post right next to the RCA output jacks, and the supplied RCA cable has both the right and left channel RCA connectors and a ground wire.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B007R9B0UQ', 'answerTime': 'Mar 19, 2014', 'unixTime': 1395212400, 'question': "Recording to computer using the USB connection: do the computer's speakers emit the music, or must it also be connected to a stereo system?", 'answer': 'You can have the music output through your computer speakers while recording. The instructions that come with the turntable explain what you need to do as far as computer and program settings to do that.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B007R9B0UQ', 'answerTime': 'Feb 12, 2014', 'unixTime': 1392192000, 'question': 'In regards to the AT 1240 turntable and the Technics 1200 is there big differences in quality build or the audio amplifiers each have.', 'answer': 'Considering that the Technics costs 4 to 5 times the Audio Technica, one would hope that might be reflected in the attention to detail in design and manufacturer, and possibly longevity. Certainly both TTs are quite rugged and heavy. The Technics has no electronics. Just two 36" cables, hard-wired to the turntable and terminating in RCA type plugs, so it\'s straight out from the cartridge. If you are going to an ampifier that has no phono pre-amp you are going to have to buy an external one. The Audio Technica has a built in phono pre-amp which can be switched off when using audio equipment that does have a phone input. (Generally the pre-amps in audio amps are better than those you find built in to most turntables.) Instead of a hard wired output cable, the A.T. has RCA plugs so that you can attach any length cable you want. It also has a USB connector/output - the Technics doesn\'t.'}
