{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B0087UQ090', 'answerTime': 'Sep 11, 2013', 'unixTime': 1378882800, 'question': 'if is good for powered speakers', 'answer': "If you're not looking to mount them too high off the ground these stand should do you just fine. My speakers are pretty heavy for unpowered ones and the were fine. So all depends in your usage situation if you want them at maximum height in the middle of an open stage, where there is wind, maybe a bad buy, otherwise I'm extremely happy with them for the price I paid."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B0087UQ090', 'answerTime': 'Jul 27, 2013', 'unixTime': 1374908400, 'question': 'What is the diameter of the poles holding up the speaker?', 'answer': 'the diameter of the poles are 2inches.. standred size for most pro speakers... however I do not recommend them I bought a pair last summer and they were a complete waste of money.. the knob to tighten the stands broke and the pin broke off and got lost too.. if your able to.. look for the brand ultimate brand speaker stand they maybe a few dollars more and can handle double the weight and are built to last!.. best of luck in the hunt! -jonathan hall- Hall Productions Disc Jockey Service , upstate new york'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B0087UQ090', 'answerTime': 'May 21, 2013', 'unixTime': 1369119600, 'question': 'what is the weight capacity for each?', 'answer': 'I don\'t know the exact weight but I am supporting a 12" Peavey. If supporting a speaker made for a stand like this I would guess less than 75lbs.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B0087UPVAO', 'answerTime': 'Jan 3, 2014', 'unixTime': 1388736000, 'question': "Any idea what the lowest height setting is? I'm hoping to get a stand I can use while sitting at my desk. Thanks!", 'answer': 'the lowest height setting of Stage Rocker Powered by Hamilton is 22 inches (56 cm.)'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B0087UPVAO', 'answerTime': 'Dec 9, 2014', 'unixTime': 1418112000, 'question': 'Would this hold a 44 key (25 in long) keyboard? Thank you!', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': "Sort of, there are 5 height settings, at its lowest setting the bars to rest the keyboard are the furthest apart (34 1/2in), so you couldn't fit the keyboard at this setting. This height is one I like because you can sit in a standard chair and play. The next height setting up, the bars are 30 1/2 in across, then the next setting they are 25 1/2in. The 4th setting they are 20in across, which would work for your keyboard, but you would have to stand to play because your keyboard would be 3 feet off the ground. It's a great keyboard stand, but I don't recommend it for your keyboard"}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B0087UPVAO', 'answerTime': 'Mar 9, 2015', 'unixTime': 1425884400, 'question': 'Will this stand hold a 64 key keyboard?', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': 'Holds mine fine.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B0087UPVAO', 'answerTime': 'Jan 17, 2015', 'unixTime': 1421481600, 'question': 'What is the maximum weight this stand has the capacity to support?', 'answer': "A Roland Fantom S88 sits on this stand since it was delivered from Amazon, I have not played on it, Also have a Peavey C8 midi controller on another Stage Rocker for about a year. Both these keyboards are heavy. The stands have held up. Been using these stands to park the keyboards and they show no strain. My Roland Fantom G8 sits on an Ultimate IQ3000 and is the one I'm currently playing, so to be up front here not sure how the Stage Rocker will perform with the heavy keys."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B0087UPVAO', 'answerTime': 'Feb 28, 2014', 'unixTime': 1393574400, 'question': 'What is the dimension of the top and bottom bars?', 'answer': 'The top bars are approximately 13 3/4". The bottom ones are approximately 19 1/2". The grooved rubber end caps on both top and bottom bars are approximately 2 1/4". Hope this helps.'}
