{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B008LMS4MK', 'answerTime': 'Dec 20, 2014', 'unixTime': 1419062400, 'question': "Has anyone run this through a sound system? I play with my church's band and I'm wondering if it'll sound ok. Would it plug into a direct box?", 'answer': "I play it through my guitar amp. The chord is damn flimsy so be careful! Sound is good, if you can use a preamp or sound processor with reverb it helps a ton. A direct box should work just fine. It's way easier and sounds better than micing it IMHO. If you push the levels of your PA system you may run into feedback issues. Run a dress rehearsal and find the best spots on stage that don't feedback. Again that chord is very flimsy, one trip and it's game over. Have a backup plan like a microphone just in case."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B008LV0L22', 'answerTime': 'May 6, 2014', 'unixTime': 1399359600, 'question': 'Can I use a regular microphone stand or do I need to purchase a stand specifically made for a theramin?', 'answer': 'A regular mic stand is exactly what you would need if you wish to mount the theremin on a stand. If the stand has a retaining nut, please remove the nut before mounting the theremin. It will slip over the threads and sit snugly on the stand. Please be aware that a stand is not required, although it can be a welcome addition. You can play this instrument as a tabletop model. Thanks!'}
