{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B009A5M518', 'answerTime': 'Oct 1, 2014', 'unixTime': 1412146800, 'question': 'how to tell witch one is witch one has a red wire one has a black wire', 'answer': 'Black is labeled bridge and Red is labeled Neck on my set.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B009A5M518', 'answerTime': 'Oct 1, 2014', 'unixTime': 1412146800, 'question': 'how to tell witch one is witch one has a red wire one has a black wire', 'answer': "It's a little tricky to explain with this set because on the Guitar that I mounted these pickups to. I only actually used Pickup, out of the two that came with the set. They sound just as Hardcore, and amazing as they should still, but whats important to remember here, is that if you're going to be using both. Pay close attention not to weither the main wire is black or red, here, because they'll both work just as fine. but when you have either the red cable, or the black one ... they have tiny shielded wires within them ... these are the important ones... one of the tiny wires is shielded with Solder, and the other one is a tiny white cable. those are the ones you wanna make sure you wire correctly when you're changing out the pickups on your guitar of choice."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B009A5M518', 'answerTime': 'Aug 28, 2014', 'unixTime': 1409209200, 'question': "What are the exact dimensions? I'm particularlly interested in the depth.", 'answer': 'They are 3 5/8" wide including the trim, by 1 13/16" wide including the trim. The depth is adjustable. There are practical concerns with the adjustability, IE: Too high will hit the strings, too low will not pick up the sound well. That said, they will intrude into the guitar body between 1/4" at the lowest to about 3/4" at their deepest. The pickups themselves are standard sized humbuckers, IE: 1 1/2" wide by 2 3/4" length by 5/8" thick.'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B009A5M518', 'answerTime': 'Aug 8, 2014', 'unixTime': 1407481200, 'question': 'are they doubled coiled?', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': 'They are double coil humbuckers, but not the coil splitting type. They are all plastic and will require shielding in the pickup cavity of your guitar (they pick up a lot of ambient 60 hz ie noise)'}
