{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B00CDINK72', 'answerTime': 'Dec 10, 2013', 'unixTime': 1386662400, 'question': 'My 10 year old wants this for xmas but I have no idea what this is! Help please!', 'answer': 'It\'s a MIDI controller. Midi controllers allow a person to interact with music software to play notes, execute commands, and perform live. Look up "Launchpad Performance" on YouTube and you\'ll see what it can do, but it NEEDS a computer/iPad(there is an app) and software to do anything. It does absolutely nothing on its own.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B00CDINK72', 'answerTime': 'Nov 24, 2013', 'unixTime': 1385280000, 'question': "I'm seriously not familiar w/these gadgets .I would like to know if it would work on windows XP. It would be for a gift. Thanks in advance!", 'answer': 'Yes, it will. However, it doesn\'t make any sort of sound on it\'s own, it needs a Digital Audio Workstation like Ableton Live, FL Studio, Cubase, etc. But it is "class-compliant" meaning it works out of the box!'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B00CDINK72', 'answerTime': 'Dec 26, 2013', 'unixTime': 1388044800, 'question': 'Does it work for mac, and does it come with the software needed to make new sounds?', 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': 'It does work for Mac. Anything that is a "class-compliant MIDI controller" will work for any computer operating system as long as there are USB ports. I use it on my 2012 iMac with Logic Pro 9 sometimes. This device was not designed to be used with Logic, but there are programs to use it with on Mac. It was originally designed for use with Ableton, and that program has a Mac version. It cannot make sounds at all. It controls software that makes sound. You should research the software first. It comes with some synths, and it can be used with the Launchpad app on iPad. Professionals are going to use it to perform music live, loop clips, trigger samples, and in some cases as a production tool.'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B00CDINK72', 'answerTime': 'May 18, 2014', 'unixTime': 1400396400, 'question': 'Can I do live preformaces with this (I.e. Hook speakers up to it and play it in public?)', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': "Nope. You can hook it up to a computer or an iPad and then hook them up t speakers. Then you're good to go."}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B00CDINK72', 'answerTime': 'Dec 14, 2014', 'unixTime': 1418544000, 'question': 'Will this work with Sony Acid Music Studio 10.0 and So do I choose how to set it up or Does it do it on its own ?', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': 'It will work, but you\'ll prolly have no lights. It\'ll send the MIDI but unless a DAW knows how to "talk back" to it, no lights will come up. If that DAW is automatically listening for MIDI data, it\'ll work out of the box. If there\'s a place to select MIDI controllers or enable them, you should only have to do that. Bottom line, it\'ll probably work, but you\'re also better off with a keyboard or control surface, in my opinion. EDIT: It does come with Ableton Live, albeit a very limited version of it. My suggestion is: Do stuff in your DAW, and then export the audio from the instruments. Load in Ableton and do all sidechaining on it (EQing before bouncing is fine). That way, should you want the LP S for Live Performances, you can use it. If you want it for production, I suggest you get something else. Novation has great keyboards with sliders and knobs, I\'d get that.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B00CDINK72', 'answerTime': 'Aug 11, 2014', 'unixTime': 1407740400, 'question': 'This comes with a basic version of Ableton. What is different about it than the full version?', 'answer': "True. It comes with a downloadable edition which is known as Ableton Live Launchpad Edition. The difference is, Ableton Live Launchpad Edition is pretty much the same thing as Ableton Live 9 Lite. It's like a demo version, but offers some more capabilities. I'd recommend buying Ableton Live 9 Suite Edition, or at least Ableton Live 8, Ableton Live 8 Suite, or Ableton Live 9."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B00CDINK72', 'answerTime': 'Dec 29, 2014', 'unixTime': 1419840000, 'question': "What's the difference between this one and the mini one (70 dollars cheaper) besides size? Is it really a crucial difference?", 'answer': "The Mini connects via Micro USB so its more likely to snap than this. But those cables aren't expensive and are easy to come by. If you are new to controllerism, though, I suggest saving the extra 70 because the smaller buttons can be a bit harder to learn on."}
